<p>Choosing the right material is the single most consequential decision in any interior project. It determines how a space feels, sounds, smells, ages, and performs over decades. Yet material knowledge is rarely taught in a single accessible place — it is scattered across technical datasheets, supplier catalogues, and years of hands-on experience.</p>
<p>This glossary changes that. Every entry explains what a material is, what makes it distinctive, how it performs in real interiors, and where it belongs. Whether you are specifying a kitchen worktop, selecting a bathroom tile, or simply curious about the difference between quartzite and quartz composite — this guide has the answer.</p>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Note</span><strong>Educational purpose:</strong> This guide is written purely for education and design literacy. Material performance varies by manufacturer, installation quality, and context. Always consult a qualified designer or contractor before specification.</div>
<p>A</p>
<h2>A — From Acoustical to Azulejo</h2>
<p>3 materials</p>
<p>Acoustical Panels</p>
<p>Panels designed to absorb sound waves and reduce echo in a space. Made from compressed mineral wool, open-cell foam, or fabric-wrapped fibreglass batts. Critical in offices, home theatres, restaurants, and open-plan living areas. Performance is measured using the NRC (Noise Reduction Coefficient) — a rating of 1.0 means total absorption. Ceiling clouds, vertical baffles, and wall-mounted panels are the three main formats.</p>
<p>CeilingWallsOfficeHome theatre</p>
<p>Acrylic (PMMA)</p>
<p>Polymethyl methacrylate — a lightweight, shatter-resistant transparent polymer often marketed as Perspex or Plexiglas. Half the weight of glass at equivalent thickness but with superior light transmission (92% vs 91% for glass). Available in high-gloss, matte, frosted, mirrored, and tinted finishes. Used in furniture faces, splashbacks, partition screens, and decorative lighting diffusers. Scratches more easily than glass but can be polished back to clarity.</p>
<p>FurnitureSplashbacksSignagePartitions</p>
<p>Azulejo Tile</p>
<p>Traditional hand-painted tin-glazed ceramic tiles originating in 13th-century Islamic Iberia, later refined in Portugal and Spain. The iconic cobalt blue and white palettes were influenced by Delft and Chinese porcelain. Each tile is painted by hand before a single transparent glaze firing, creating subtle tonal variation impossible to replicate industrially. Now available in multicolour contemporary interpretations from artisan studios worldwide. A statement of craft and cultural heritage in any interior.</p>
<p>BathroomKitchenFeature wallsHeritage projects</p>
<p>B</p>
<h2>B — From Bamboo to Brick</h2>
<p>3 materials</p>
<p>Bamboo</p>
<p>Technically a grass, bamboo is one of the fastest-growing plants on earth — some species grow 90cm per day. Harvested in 3–5 years versus 25+ for hardwoods. Tensile strength rivals mild steel. Used as solid strand-woven flooring (the hardest bamboo format), wall cladding, and furniture. Carbonised bamboo is darker and slightly softer than natural. Important caveat: binding adhesives in bamboo products can off-gas VOCs — specify low-emission certified products.</p>
<p>FlooringCladdingFurnitureSustainable</p>
<p>Brick (Exposed Interior)</p>
<p>Kiln-fired clay units used structurally and decoratively. Exposed brick in interiors adds texture, warmth, and industrial or rustic character. Varieties include engineering brick (very dense, low water absorption — used near ground level), facing brick (aesthetic quality, used for visible surfaces), and handmade brick with beautiful natural size variation and surface texture. Brick slips — thin brick veneers at 20–25mm — allow the aesthetic without structural weight. Seal exposed interior brick to prevent dust and moisture transmission.</p>
<p>Feature wallsFireplacesIndustrial style</p>
<p>Batten Board / Slat Wall</p>
<p>Thin strips of timber (25–50mm wide, 12–18mm thick) fixed at regular intervals vertically or horizontally on walls or ceilings. The rhythmic repetition of light and shadow creates extraordinary visual depth and texture. A signature element of Japandi, Scandi, and contemporary biophilic design. Can be painted, stained, oiled, or left raw. Timber species choices include oak, ash, pine, and MDF for painted applications. Also provides a modest degree of acoustic dampening.</p>
<p>WallsCeilingsHeadboardsTV walls</p>
<p>C</p>
<h2>C — From Concrete to Cork</h2>
<p>4 materials</p>
<p>Concrete (Polished / Microcement)</p>
<p>A composite of Portland cement, aggregate, and water. Polished concrete floors deliver seamless, hygienic surfaces with excellent thermal mass for passive heating. GFRC (Glass Fibre Reinforced Concrete) creates lightweight decorative panels and worktops. Microcement is a thin-coat polymer-modified cement applied at just 2–3mm over existing surfaces — no demolition required. Both require professional sealing and occasional re-sealing. Prone to hairline cracking over time, which many designers consider a desirable characteristic.</p>
<p>FloorsWorktopsWallsBathrooms</p>
<p>Cork</p>
<p>Harvested from the bark of the cork oak (Quercus suber) every 9–12 years without felling the tree — making it genuinely renewable. 50% air by volume, giving it exceptional acoustic and thermal insulation properties. Naturally antimicrobial, hypoallergenic, and fire-resistant (chars rather than burning). Used as flooring tiles, wall tiles, and underlays. Modern cork flooring is available with hardwearing surface treatments that maintain the warmth while improving durability. An increasingly popular choice in sustainable luxury design.</p>
<p>FlooringWall tilesInsulationAcoustic</p>
<p>Ceramic Tile</p>
<p>Fired clay tiles with a surface glaze for water resistance. One of humanity's oldest interior materials — found in ancient Egyptian, Roman, and Islamic architecture. Modern ceramic tiles use digital inkjet printing to replicate marble, wood, concrete, fabric, and geometric patterns at extraordinary resolution. Wall ceramics fire at lower temperatures than floor tiles; always check PEI hardness rating for floor use. Grout joint width profoundly affects the final aesthetic — large formats with minimal grout read as seamless stone.</p>
<p>BathroomKitchenFloorsWalls</p>
<p>Copper</p>
<p>A warm reddish-orange metal with natural antimicrobial properties — a scientifically documented fact that makes it excellent for door handles and worktops. Develops a complex dark patina (oxidation) over time that many consider beautiful. Used in range hoods, splashbacks, pendant lights, bathtubs, and decorative hardware. Unlacquered copper patinates naturally; lacquered copper preserves the new-metal appearance. Cannot be used with bleach-based cleaners as chlorine attacks the metal.</p>
<p>HardwareLightingSplashbacksBespoke</p>
<div class="callout callout--tip"><span class="callout__title">Tip</span>💡 <strong>Pro tip:</strong> Polished concrete amplifies sound significantly. In any room with concrete floors, always pair with rugs, upholstered furniture, and ceiling acoustic treatments to maintain conversational comfort. The thermal mass benefit is real — in passive solar homes, polished concrete floors absorb heat during the day and release it at night.</div>
<p>D</p>
<h2>D — From Drywall to Douglas Fir</h2>
<p>2 materials</p>
<p>Drywall (Plasterboard / Gypsum Board)</p>
<p>Gypsum mineral sandwiched between two sheets of paper, creating panels used to construct interior walls and ceilings rapidly. The dominant wall construction method in modern building. Available in standard, moisture-resistant (green board), fire-resistant (Type X — up to 2-hour fire rating), soundproofing (acoustic grade), and flexible (curved wall) types. Finished with joint compound and skim plaster for a paintable surface. Repairs are straightforward compared to plaster-and-lath construction.</p>
<p>WallsCeilingsPartitions</p>
<p>Douglas Fir</p>
<p>A strong, straight-grained softwood — one of the most structurally important timbers in North American construction. Its reddish-brown heartwood and distinctive grain pattern make it popular for exposed structural beams, flooring, and panelling in rustic, mid-century, and craftsman interiors. Accepts stains and oils beautifully. Slightly resinous — can bleed through certain oil-based paints. Reclaimed Douglas Fir from deconstructed warehouses and factories has a following among designers who prize its aged patina.</p>
<p>BeamsFlooringPanellingStructural</p>
<p>E</p>
<h2>E — From Epoxy to Engineered Wood</h2>
<p>2 materials</p>
<p>Epoxy Resin</p>
<p>A two-part thermosetting polymer (resin + hardener) that cures to a hard, glossy, seamless surface. Applied as self-levelling floor coatings, worktop surfaces, and artistic furniture pieces. Can encapsulate objects, pigments, metallic powders, and aggregates within the material. Extremely chemical-resistant and highly durable once cured. UV-stable formulations are required for rooms with direct sunlight or the surface yellows over time. Achieves 60–80 Shore D hardness — harder than most natural stones.</p>
<p>FloorsWorktopsFurniture artIndustrial</p>
<p>Engineered Wood (Plywood / MDF / LVL)</p>
<p>A family of manufactured wood composites: plywood (cross-laminated veneer sheets), MDF (wood fibres bonded under pressure), OSB (oriented strand board), and LVL (Laminated Veneer Lumber for structural beams). Far more dimensionally stable than solid timber — resists warping and shrinkage. Birch plywood has become an aesthetic choice in Scandinavian interiors for its clean, pale grain. All engineered woods are available in structural and decorative grades with varying adhesive emissions levels.</p>
<p>FurnitureCabinetrySubfloorsStructure</p>
<p>F</p>
<h2>F — From Fabric to Feature Wallpaper</h2>
<p>3 materials</p>
<p>Fabric &amp; Upholstery Textiles</p>
<p>The collective term for woven, knitted, and non-woven textiles used on furniture, curtains, and walls. Key performance metrics: abrasion resistance measured in Martindale rubs (domestic use requires 15,000+ rubs; contract use 40,000+), pilling resistance, colourfastness to light, and fire rating (Crib 5 for contract upholstery in the UK). Natural fabrics breathe better; synthetics offer superior durability. Boucle (looped yarn), bouclé, performance velvet, and indoor-outdoor fabrics are current specification favourites.</p>
<p>UpholsteryCurtainsWall panelsCushions</p>
<p>Fibreglass (GRP)</p>
<p>Glass-reinforced polymer: glass fibres embedded in a polyester or epoxy resin matrix. Exceptionally lightweight (one-quarter the weight of steel at comparable strength), mouldable into complex curves and forms impossible in other materials. Used in baths, shower trays, door canopies, decorative mouldings, and architectural panels. Can replicate stone, plaster, and timber textures convincingly. Resistant to water, most chemicals, and corrosion. Colour-stable in UV-resistant grades.</p>
<p>BathsMouldingsPanelsArchitectural</p>
<p>Feature Wallpaper</p>
<p>Decorative wall coverings available in paper, vinyl, grasscloth, fabric-backed, metallic foil, and digital-print substrates. Type 1 vinyl (light commercial) and Type 2 (heavy commercial) wallcoverings have standardised performance ratings. Grasscloth woven from natural jute or sisal adds organic warmth and tactile texture. Hand-blocked and screen-printed wallpapers from independent makers command premium prices for their artisanal character. Digital wallpaper printing now allows photographic murals at any scale.</p>
<p>BedroomsHallwaysFeature wallsCommercial</p>
<p>G</p>
<h2>G — From Glass to Granite</h2>
<p>2 materials</p>
<p>Glass (Architectural)</p>
<p>Float glass, toughened (tempered) glass, laminated glass, wired glass, and low-iron glass each serve distinct purposes. Toughened glass is 4–5× stronger than float glass and breaks safely into small rounded fragments. Laminated glass (two layers bonded with PVB interlayer) holds together when broken — required for overhead glazing and floors. Low-iron glass has less than 0.01% iron oxide — virtually no green tint, giving a water-clear appearance ideal for splashbacks. Switchable (smart) glass transitions from opaque to transparent with an electrical current.</p>
<p>PartitionsBalustradesSplashbacksDoors</p>
<p>Granite</p>
<p>An intrusive igneous rock formed by slow crystallisation of magma beneath the earth's surface. The result is a dense, extremely hard stone (Mohs 6–7) with a characteristic speckled, granular appearance. One of the hardest worktop materials available — highly scratch-resistant. However, granite is porous and must be sealed annually to prevent oil and wine staining. Each slab is geologically unique. Popular finishes: polished (mirror-bright), honed (matte), leathered (textured). Black Galaxy, Blue Pearl, and Kashmir White are perennial interior specifications.</p>
<p>WorktopsFloorsFeature wallsBathroom</p>
<p>H</p>
<h2>H — From Hardwood to Hemp</h2>
<p>2 materials</p>
<p>Hardwood (Solid &amp; Engineered)</p>
<p>Timber from deciduous trees including oak, walnut, ash, teak, maple, cherry, and iroko. Denser and more durable than softwood. Solid hardwood floors can be sanded and refinished 5–7 times over a 100+ year lifespan — a genuinely lifetime material. The Janka hardness scale rates scratch resistance: Brazilian Cherry (2350 lbf) is nearly four times harder than American Cherry (950 lbf). Quarter-sawn hardwood — sliced perpendicular to the growth rings — produces the most dimensionally stable boards with distinctive ray figure on oak and sycamore.</p>
<p>FlooringFurnitureJoineryStaircase</p>
<p>Hemp &amp; Hempcrete</p>
<p>Hemp fibres (the woody core of the Cannabis sativa plant, called hurds or shiv) are mixed with hydraulic lime binder and water to create hempcrete — a lightweight, breathable, carbon-sequestering insulation and walling material. Hempcrete is not load-bearing but used as infill within a structural frame. It regulates internal humidity naturally, resists mould and pests, and improves in strength as the lime continues carbonating for decades. Hemp textiles are increasingly used for rugs, curtains, and natural upholstery fabrics.</p>
<p>InsulationWallsTextilesSustainable</p>
<p>I</p>
<h2>I — From Iron to Italian Marble</h2>
<p>2 materials</p>
<p>Iron (Wrought &amp; Cast)</p>
<p>Wrought iron is worked by hand whilst hot — malleable, fibrous, and weldable. Cast iron is molten iron poured into moulds — rigid, brittle, and excellent for complex forms. Interior applications include radiators (cast iron has outstanding thermal mass — heating and cooling slowly for even warmth), balustrades, fire surrounds, range cookers, and decorative hardware. Both develop a beautiful dark patina. Both require oil or wax protection against rust. Victorian-era cast iron radiators are actively sought for period and contemporary interiors alike.</p>
<p>RadiatorsHardwareRailingsFire surrounds</p>
<p>Italian Marble (Carrara &amp; Calacatta)</p>
<p>Metamorphic limestone quarried in the Apuan Alps of Tuscany. Carrara marble — grey veined on a white background — has been the material of choice for sculpture and architecture since ancient Rome. Calacatta is quarried from a more limited area of the same mountains; its bolder gold or grey veining on a brighter white background makes it rarer and typically two to three times more expensive. Both are porous (require annual sealing), and susceptible to acid etching from lemon juice, wine, and many cleaners. The etching dulls the polish — a fact of living with marble, not a defect.</p>
<p>WorktopsBathroomsFloorsFireplace</p>
<p>J</p>
<h2>J — From Jute to Jasper</h2>
<p>2 materials</p>
<p>Jute</p>
<p>A long, soft, shiny bast fibre from the Corchorus plant, grown primarily in Bangladesh and India. The second most produced natural fibre after cotton. 100% biodegradable and one of the most affordable natural fibres. Woven into rugs, cushion covers, baskets, and wall hangings. The slightly coarse texture improves with use as fibres soften. Not recommended as a primary floor covering in very high-traffic areas — use flat-weave or tightly-spun jute blends instead. Also used as backing material for tufted carpets.</p>
<p>RugsBasketsWall artBiodegradable</p>
<p>Jasper Stone</p>
<p>An opaque, fine-grained variety of chalcedony (microcrystalline quartz) occurring in red, yellow, brown, and green through iron oxide impurities. Used as decorative stone inlays, feature cladding panels, and in high-end bathroom feature walls. Each piece has a unique pattern of swirling colour. Polished jasper has a vitreous lustre. Historically used in pietra dura (Italian stone mosaic) work in Mughal palaces and Italian churches — prized by those who appreciate traditional stone craft.</p>
<p>Feature wallsInlayDecorative</p>
<p>K</p>
<h2>K — From Kraft Paper Panel to Kwila</h2>
<p>2 materials</p>
<p>Kraft Paper Composite Panel</p>
<p>Multiple layers of kraft paper impregnated with phenolic resin, bonded under high heat and extreme pressure to form a dense, rigid composite material. Sold under brand names such as Richlite, Durat, and paper composite. Warm mid-brown tones, fine linear grain pattern, and extremely matte surface. Heat-resistant to 160°C. Used for kitchen worktops, wall cladding, and furniture surfaces. A genuinely sustainable material — no formaldehyde off-gassing, made largely from waste paper fibre.</p>
<p>WorktopsCladdingFurnitureSustainable</p>
<p>Kwila (Merbau)</p>
<p>An extremely dense tropical hardwood from SE Asia, the Pacific Islands, and Australia. Janka hardness of 1925 lbf — one of the hardest decking and flooring timbers available. Deep reddish-brown heartwood with interlocked grain. Naturally high in oil content providing outstanding weather and insect resistance. The resins can stain light-coloured materials during installation — pre-rinse boards before laying. FSC (Forest Stewardship Council) certification is essential when specifying tropical hardwoods to ensure responsible sourcing.</p>
<p>FlooringStairsDeckingExternal</p>
<p>L</p>
<h2>L — From Leather to Limewash</h2>
<p>3 materials</p>
<p>Limestone</p>
<p>A sedimentary rock composed primarily of calcium carbonate from marine organisms. Softer than granite (Mohs 3–4) but offering warmer cream, beige, and grey tones. Tumbled limestone tiles have softened edges and an aged appearance; honed limestone is flat and matte; brushed limestone has a textured, tactile surface. Must be sealed against water and acid penetration. Underfloor heating pairs beautifully with limestone — the stone is comfortable underfoot when warm. Popular in Provençal, Mediterranean, and contemporary natural interior styles.</p>
<p>FloorsWallsFireplacesTraditional</p>
<p>Leather (Full-grain &amp; Top-grain)</p>
<p>Full-grain leather retains the complete outer surface of the hide — all natural markings, scars, and grain variation intact. It is the strongest, most breathable, and most expensive grade. Develops a unique patina over decades of use, improving in character. Top-grain leather has the surface lightly sanded and coated — more uniform appearance, slightly less breathable. Corrected-grain and bonded leather are lower-quality manufactured products with greatly reduced longevity. For contract upholstery, specify leather with a minimum 1.2mm thickness and a surface treatment rating for wet rub fastness.</p>
<p>UpholsteryWall panelsAccessoriesLuxury</p>
<p>Limewash Paint</p>
<p>A breathable, naturally anti-fungal wall finish made from slaked lime (calcium hydroxide) mixed with water and earth pigments. Applied in multiple thin coats — each layer is burnished or left textured to build depth. The mottled, layered effect changes character dramatically with natural light throughout the day: flat at midday, deeply dimensional at dawn and dusk. Used continuously for more than 2,000 years; increasingly embraced in biophilic and organic interiors. Breathes better than any synthetic paint — essential on historic masonry walls.</p>
<p>WallsCeilingsHistoricBiophilic</p>
<p>M</p>
<h2>M — From Marble to Microcement</h2>
<p>3 materials</p>
<p>Marble</p>
<p>Metamorphic limestone crystallised under heat and pressure. The resulting calcite crystals reflect light, giving polished marble its unique luminous depth. Veining formed by mineral impurities during metamorphism — no two slabs are identical. Relatively soft (Mohs 3–4) — susceptible to scratching, etching (acid attack), and staining. In kitchens this is managed through honed rather than polished finishes (less visible etching) and diligent sealing. Iconic stone varieties: Carrara (grey veined), Calacatta (gold veined), Nero Marquina (black with white veins), Emperador (brown), and Verde Guatemala (green).</p>
<p>BathroomsKitchen islandsFoyersFireplace</p>
<p>MDF (Medium-Density Fibreboard)</p>
<p>Wood fibres blended with urea-formaldehyde or PMDI resin and bonded under heat and pressure into dense, consistent panels with no grain direction. Smooth, stable, and ideal for painting and machining — routed profiles hold sharp edges unlike solid wood. The material of choice for painted cabinetry, skirting boards, architraves, and door furniture. Moisture-resistant grades (MR-MDF) are suitable for kitchen and bathroom use. Specify E0 or E1 emission class boards for low formaldehyde off-gassing in occupied spaces.</p>
<p>CabinetrySkirtingJoineryPainted finishes</p>
<p>Microcement (Thin-coat Concrete)</p>
<p>A polymer-modified cementitious coating applied at 2–3mm thickness directly over existing tiles, concrete, screed, or plasterboard without demolition. Creates a seamless, joint-free surface with the aesthetic of polished concrete. Available in a palette of 60–200 colours depending on manufacturer, and in matte or satin finishes. Requires skilled application in at least 3 layers plus sealing — poor application leads to cracking or delamination. The sealer requires renewal every 12–24 months in wet areas. Excellent for bathroom walls where traditional tiling would be prohibitively thick.</p>
<p>FloorsWallsBathroomsRenovation</p>
<div class="callout callout--tip"><span class="callout__title">Tip</span>💡 <strong>Marble vs Quartzite vs Engineered Quartz:</strong> These three materials are frequently confused. Marble is the most beautiful but least durable. Quartzite is a natural stone that looks similar to marble but is far harder and acid-resistant. Engineered quartz is a manufactured product — non-porous, never needs sealing, most consistent appearance. The choice between them depends entirely on how you live and your tolerance for maintenance.</div>
<p>N</p>
<h2>N — From Natural Stone to Nickel</h2>
<p>2 materials</p>
<p>Natural Stone (Overview)</p>
<p>The collective classification for quarried geological materials: marble, granite, slate, travertine, limestone, sandstone, quartzite, onyx, and schist. Each has unique hardness (Mohs scale), porosity (water absorption %), thermal conductivity, and aesthetic character. The defining feature of all natural stone: no two pieces are ever identical. Natural stone is genuinely the most sustainable interior material — quarried once, shaped once, and capable of lasting for centuries or millennia without replacement. The Pantheon in Rome is surfaced with natural stone that is nearly 2,000 years old.</p>
<p>All surfacesFloorsWallsWorktops</p>
<p>Nickel (Satin &amp; Brushed)</p>
<p>Nickel plating applied over brass or steel substrate, then mechanically treated to create satin (fine, multi-directional texture) or brushed (linear, unidirectional texture) finishes. Both have a silver-grey tonality warmer than chrome, cooler than brass. Highly resistant to fingerprints and water spots. One of the most enduring hardware finishes — neither fashion-forward nor retro. Pairs beautifully with both warm timber and cool stone palettes. PVD (Physical Vapour Deposition) nickel coatings are significantly harder and more resistant than traditional plating.</p>
<p>HardwareFixturesTapsDoor furniture</p>
<p>O</p>
<h2>O — From Oak to Onyx</h2>
<p>2 materials</p>
<p>Oak (European &amp; American White)</p>
<p>The single most widely specified hardwood in European and North American interiors. Extremely durable, dimensionally stable when dried correctly, and accepting of all finishes from oil to lacquer to paint. European oak (Quercus robur / petraea) has a slightly wider grain and more visible medullary rays than American White Oak (Quercus alba). Quarter-sawn oak exposes the medullary rays as a distinctive fleck pattern — a premium specification associated with Arts &amp; Crafts and contemporary minimalist interiors. Smoked, fumed, wire-brushed, and character-grade oaks each offer distinctive aesthetics.</p>
<p>FlooringCabinetryFurnitureJoinery</p>
<p>Onyx</p>
<p>A banded variety of chalcedony (microcrystalline quartz) occurring in white, honey, green, red, and black colourways. The defining characteristic of white and honey onyx: translucency. Backlit onyx panels glow with extraordinary warmth and depth — a technique used in luxury hotel bars, reception counters, and meditation rooms. Relatively soft (Mohs 6.5–7) — not suitable for kitchen worktops or high-traffic floors. Often mounted on a fibreglass or aluminium backing for structural support. The drama-to-cost ratio is exceptional compared to other luxury stone choices.</p>
<p>Feature wallsBacklit panelsBar frontsLuxury</p>
<p>P</p>
<h2>P — From Porcelain to Polished Plaster</h2>
<p>3 materials</p>
<p>Porcelain Tile (Large Format)</p>
<p>Fired at 1200–1400°C from a refined clay and mineral mix, producing a material denser and harder than ceramic. Water absorption below 0.5% — suitable for wet areas without any sealing. Large-format slabs (1200×2400mm, 1600×3200mm) clad entire walls or floors with only 2–3 joints, creating a near-seamless contemporary aesthetic. Through-body porcelain maintains the surface colour through the full tile depth — chips are invisible. The dominant premium floor and wall material of the 21st century, with digital printing enabling convincing stone, wood, and concrete replications.</p>
<p>FloorsWallsOutdoorWet rooms</p>
<p>Venetian Plaster (Polished Plaster)</p>
<p>A family of Italian wall finish techniques using marble dust and slaked lime, applied in multiple thin coats and progressively burnished. The most common modern variants: Marmorino (fine, polished — resembles marble), Tadelakt (Moroccan origin — extremely dense and water-resistant, used inside shower enclosures), and Stucco Lustro (the most highly burnished — mirror-like sheen). The depth of layered polish creates an optical quality unlike any painted surface. Application is a skilled trade — results vary enormously with the craftsperson's expertise. Often takes 3–7 days per room.</p>
<p>WallsCeilingsBathroomsLuxury</p>
<p>Plywood (Structural &amp; Decorative)</p>
<p>Manufactured from thin veneer sheets bonded with alternating grain direction, giving equal strength in both panel directions — unlike solid wood. Birch plywood with its very fine, consistent pale grain has become an aesthetic choice in Scandi, industrial, and contemporary minimalist interiors. Structural grades include WBP (weather and boil proof) for humid conditions and marine plywood for wet environments. Hardwood-face plywood veneered in oak, walnut, or ash offers the aesthetics of solid timber at a fraction of the cost and with superior stability.</p>
<p>FurnitureCladdingCabinetryStructure</p>
<div class="callout callout--tip"><span class="callout__title">Tip</span>💡 <strong>Pro tip for large-format porcelain:</strong> When specifying tiles larger than 1200mm, ensure the subfloor is flat to within 3mm per 3-metre straight edge. Any flex or undulation will crack the tile at grout joints over time. Use flexible adhesive (S2 classification) with full coverage — no spot bedding. Large-format tiles are unforgiving of substrate imperfections.</div>
<p>Q</p>
<h2>Q — From Quartzite to Quartz Composite</h2>
<p>2 materials</p>
<p>Quartzite (Natural Stone)</p>
<p>A metamorphic rock formed when sandstone is subjected to intense heat and pressure, fusing the quartz grains into an interlocking crystalline mosaic. One of the hardest natural stones (Mohs 7–8) — harder than granite. White and grey quartzite varieties bear a strong visual resemblance to marble but are dramatically more durable and acid-resistant. Taj Mahal, Super White, and Calacatta Macaubas are popular interior specifications. Frequently misidentified by suppliers — always test with a drop of acid: true quartzite will not react; marble will fizz and etch immediately.</p>
<p>WorktopsFloorsCladdingDurable</p>
<p>Quartz Composite (Engineered Quartz)</p>
<p>93–95% natural quartz crystals bound with 5–7% polymer resins and pigments, manufactured under vacuum vibro-compaction. Non-porous — never needs sealing. Stain-resistant against wine, oil, and coffee. Consistent pattern and colour — no natural slab variation. Scratch-resistant but not scratch-proof. Critical limitation: cannot withstand sustained direct heat above 150°C — hot pans directly on the surface can cause thermal shock cracking and resin discolouration. Brands include Silestone, Caesarstone, Compac, and Cosentino. The practical, lower-maintenance alternative to natural stone for family kitchens.</p>
<p>WorktopsBathroomsLow maintenance</p>
<p>R</p>
<h2>R — From Rattan to Rubber Flooring</h2>
<p>2 materials</p>
<p>Rattan &amp; Cane</p>
<p>Rattan is a climbing palm vine; cane refers to the outer skin of the rattan stalk, peeled and woven. Both are lightweight, flexible, and incredibly strong relative to their weight. Solid rattan poles form chair frames and furniture structures; split rattan or woven cane is used for seat backs, door panels, and lighting shades. Rattan cannot be grown in temperate climates — most comes from Indonesia and Malaysia. Steamed to bend and mould before drying. A key material in Coastal, Boho, and Tropical interior styles. Excellent sustainability credentials compared to plastics.</p>
<p>FurnitureLightingPanelsAccessories</p>
<p>Rubber Flooring</p>
<p>Vulcanised natural or synthetic rubber available as interlocking tiles, rolled sheets, and loose-lay tiles. Originally a purely industrial and sports material, rubber flooring has entered residential and hospitality design for its exceptional durability, anti-fatigue properties (reduces joint stress on hard floors by 30–40%), and natural slip resistance. Available in a broad colour palette and several surface textures. Sound-absorbing — reduces impact noise transmission between floors. Resistant to water, oils, and most chemicals. Modern fine-textured rubber tiles are virtually indistinguishable from premium linoleum at a glance.</p>
<p>KitchensGymsCommercialAnti-fatigue</p>
<p>S</p>
<h2>S — From Slate to Sintered Stone</h2>
<p>3 materials</p>
<p>Slate</p>
<p>A fine-grained metamorphic rock that cleaves naturally into thin, flat sheets along the foliation planes. This natural cleavage creates a textured, slightly uneven surface that is inherently non-slip — a practical advantage over polished stones in wet areas and hearth surrounds. Available in charcoal, grey, green, purple, and red depending on mineral composition. Welsh slate (Penrhyn Blue/Grey) and Brazilian slate (Black) are the most commonly specified interior grades. Low porosity — rarely needs sealing. The quintessential material for traditional British kitchen floors, log burner hearths, and billiard tables.</p>
<p>FloorsHearthsCladdingTraditional</p>
<p>Sintered Stone (Dekton / Neolith)</p>
<p>Manufactured by compressing a blend of 20+ natural minerals (quartz, glass, porcelain) under 25,000 tonnes of pressure at 1200°C — accelerating the geological processes that form natural stone from millions of years to a few hours. The resulting material is virtually impervious to heat (up to 300°C), UV radiation (suitable for outdoor use), frost, acids, and scratching. Available in thicknesses from 4mm (wall cladding) to 30mm (structural worktops). Available in formats up to 3200×1440mm. The best technical performance of any surface material currently available.</p>
<p>WorktopsCladdingOutdoorHigh-performance</p>
<p>Steel (Stainless &amp; Corten)</p>
<p>Stainless steel grade 304 (18% chromium, 8% nickel) is the standard for domestic kitchens and food preparation. Grade 316 (with molybdenum) provides superior corrosion resistance for marine environments and salt exposure. Corten (weathering steel) develops a stable, self-protecting patina of rich rust-orange that does not continue to corrode — used in fire surrounds, exterior cladding, and statement planters. A brushed finish (No. 4 satin) on stainless steel is the most forgiving in kitchens — fingerprints and scratches blend into the texture over time.</p>
<p>KitchenStructureFurnitureFireplace</p>
<p>T</p>
<h2>T — From Timber to Terrazzo</h2>
<p>3 materials</p>
<p>Timber (Solid, Reclaimed)</p>
<p>The original interior material. Solid timber in floors, exposed beams, joinery, and furniture brings organic warmth, acoustic softness, and tactile character that no manufactured material can fully replicate. Timber absorbs and releases moisture — it must be acclimatised on-site before installation and specified with appropriate moisture content for the environment. Reclaimed timber from barns, factories, and demolished buildings is increasingly prized: old-growth wood with tight growth rings is denser, more stable, and more beautiful than modern fast-grown equivalents. Each reclaimed board tells a story no new material can match.</p>
<p>FloorsBeamsFurnitureJoinery</p>
<p>Travertine</p>
<p>A form of limestone deposited by mineral springs, particularly hot springs. The defining characteristic: natural voids and channels formed by carbon dioxide bubbles escaping during deposition. These can be left open (unfilled, rustic — cavities trap dirt but add raw character) or filled with grout or resin and honed for a contemporary, smooth surface. Warm cream, walnut, and ivory tones. Sensitive to acid and requires sealing. The classic material of Roman architecture (the Colosseum is built largely from travertine) and the defining stone of Mediterranean luxury villas and spas.</p>
<p>BathroomsFloorsFeature wallsMediterranean</p>
<p>Terrazzo</p>
<p>An ancient Venetian flooring technique: marble, granite, glass, or shell chips embedded in a cement or epoxy resin binder, poured in place or cast as tiles, then ground and polished smooth. Originated in 15th-century Venice where workers used marble offcuts from palace construction projects. Traditional cement terrazzo is poured in situ and ground across multiple grit stages. Modern epoxy terrazzo is resin-based — brighter colours, lighter weight, higher sheen, and zero porosity. Custom chip mixes, colours, and brass dividing strips create bespoke patterns. One of the most durable floor materials available — airport terrazzo floors show no meaningful wear after 30+ years of millions of footsteps.</p>
<p>FloorsWorktopsTilesCommercial</p>
<p>U</p>
<h2>U — From Underlay to Ultra-Compact Surface</h2>
<p>2 materials</p>
<p>Underlay (Flooring)</p>
<p>The hidden layer installed beneath the finish floor that provides cushioning, thermal insulation, acoustic dampening, and moisture protection. Underlay type must be matched to the floor above: foam for laminate, rubber crumb for carpet, cork for engineered timber, and DPM (damp-proof membrane) where moisture is present. TOG rating measures thermal insulation — relevant for underfloor-heated rooms where high-TOG underlays impede heat transfer. Acoustic underlays are critical in apartments and multi-storey buildings to reduce impact noise transmission between floors. Never an area to economise — poor underlay degrades both the floor performance and longevity.</p>
<p>CarpetLaminateEngineered woodAcoustic</p>
<p>Ultra-Compact Surface (Lapitec / Laminam)</p>
<p>Large-format sintered ceramic or porcelain panels produced at extreme temperature and pressure to achieve properties superior to standard porcelain tiles. Available in gauges from 3mm (wall cladding) to 20mm (structural floors and worktops). Outstanding UV stability — maintains colour outdoors indefinitely. Suitable for kitchen worktops, bathroom walls, exterior facades, and floor heating systems. Brands include Lapitec (fully sintered stone), Laminam (large-format sintered ceramic), and Fiandre. A premium specification positioned at the intersection of technical performance and large-scale aesthetic impact.</p>
<p>WorktopsCladdingFacadesTechnical</p>
<p>V</p>
<h2>V — From Vinyl to Velvet</h2>
<p>2 materials</p>
<p>Vinyl / LVT Flooring</p>
<p>Luxury Vinyl Tile (LVT) and Luxury Vinyl Plank (LVP) represent a genuine revolution in affordable flooring. Constructed of multiple layers: a rigid core (WPC — wood-plastic composite, or SPC — stone-plastic composite), a photographic décor layer (can replicate stone or wood with remarkable fidelity), and a transparent wear layer. Completely waterproof — suitable for bathrooms and kitchens. Wear layer thickness determines longevity: 0.2mm (domestic low-traffic), 0.3–0.5mm (domestic high-traffic), 0.55–1.0mm (commercial). Warm, quiet, and comfortable underfoot compared to stone or ceramic.</p>
<p>KitchensBathroomsHigh trafficBudget</p>
<p>Velvet</p>
<p>A cut-pile woven fabric with a characteristic soft sheen that changes tonally depending on the viewing angle and direction of pile. Silk velvet is the most lustrous and historically prestigious; cotton velvet the most breathable; viscose velvet the most dramatic in sheen. Polyester and performance velvets are the most durable for upholstery. Chenille velvet (looped rather than cut pile) has a more casual, textured hand. All velvets require careful maintenance: always clean and brush in the pile direction to avoid permanent crush marks. A defining fabric of maximalist, jewel-toned interior aesthetics.</p>
<p>UpholsteryCurtainsCushionsLuxury</p>
<p>W</p>
<h2>W — From Walnut to Wool</h2>
<p>2 materials</p>
<p>Walnut (Black &amp; European)</p>
<p>One of the most prestigious cabinet and furniture timbers in Western design. American Black Walnut (Juglans nigra) has a deep chocolate-brown heartwood with straight to slightly wavy grain — its natural colour darkens when oiled. European Walnut (Juglans regia) tends to have more figure variation, lighter sapwood streaks, and a slightly softer tone. Both have a Janka hardness of approximately 1010 lbf — hard enough for flooring but soft enough to be shaped with fine tools. The natural oils in walnut make it self-sealing in many applications. A material that ages with extraordinary grace.</p>
<p>FurnitureCabinetryFlooringLuxury</p>
<p>Wool (Carpet &amp; Upholstery)</p>
<p>A natural protein fibre from sheep with a unique combination of thermal regulation, moisture wicking, and resilience. Wool fibre has a natural crimp that makes it spring back from compression — pure wool carpets maintain their pile depth far longer than synthetics. Naturally fire-resistant (requires 25% oxygen to combust; standard air is 21%) — self-extinguishes and does not melt. The most acoustically absorbent flooring material available. New Zealand and British wools have distinct fibre characteristics prized by different carpet manufacturers. Specify with a moth-proofing treatment for wool carpets in domestic use.</p>
<p>CarpetRugsUpholsteryAcoustic</p>
<p>X</p>
<h2>X — From Xerographic Print to Xenolith</h2>
<p>1 material</p>
<p>UV-Direct Print on Surfaces</p>
<p>Modern UV flatbed and roll-to-roll inkjet printing technology allows photographic-quality imagery to be printed directly onto glass, ceramic tiles, metal panels, timber boards, and fabric at scale. The UV-cured inks bond mechanically to the substrate surface and are resistant to abrasion and moisture once cured. Applications include bespoke feature wall artwork, custom tile patterns, branded commercial interiors, and personalised furniture fronts. This technology democratises bespoke: custom colour-matched tiles or photographic murals are now accessible outside of luxury project budgets.</p>
<p>Feature wallsBespoke tilesCommercialSignage</p>
<p>Y</p>
<h2>Y — From Yorkshire Stone to Yellow Brick</h2>
<p>1 material</p>
<p>Yorkshire Stone &amp; Sandstone Flags</p>
<p>Sandstone and millstone grit quarried in Northern England — some of Britain's finest flooring stone. Buff, grey, and blue-grey colourways. Naturally textured sawn or riven (cleaved) surfaces provide excellent slip resistance. Hard, frost-resistant, and highly durable — Yorkshire stone flags in farmhouse kitchens have survived 200+ years of continuous use. Reclaimed flags carry an additional layer of history: worn thresholds, notched edges, and varied hues from decades of use. Particularly suited to period properties, orangeries, and rooms opening to garden terraces where indoor-outdoor continuity is the design goal.</p>
<p>FloorsExternalFireplacesPeriod homes</p>
<p>Z</p>
<h2>Z — From Zellige to Zinc</h2>
<p>2 materials</p>
<p>Zellige Tile</p>
<p>Hand-cut, hand-glazed terracotta tiles produced in Fez and Marrakech, Morocco. The manufacturing process is unchanged for over 1,000 years: terracotta bisque is glazed, fired, then cut to shape with a mattoir (adze) by specialist craftspeople. Each tile is unique — slight variations in thickness, glaze density, colour tonality, and edge geometry are inherent to the process, not defects. These irregularities create an extraordinary depth and shimmer when tiled en masse, as each tile catches light differently. Now one of the single most in-demand tile formats globally for luxury bathrooms, kitchens, and restaurant interiors. Installation requires a skilled tiler — standard methods do not account for the thickness variation.</p>
<p>BathroomsKitchensFeature wallsLuxury</p>
<p>Zinc</p>
<p>A bluish-grey metal used in sheet form for worktops, splashbacks, bar surfaces, and cladding panels. Develops a beautiful natural matte patina (zinc carbonate / zinc hydroxide) within weeks of installation — similar in character to aged pewter. Antimicrobial properties documented by independent research make it suitable for food preparation areas. Softer than stainless steel (Brinell hardness ~412 vs 200 for 304 SS) — marks and dents accumulate over time and are considered part of the material's character narrative. Traditional material for French bistro bar tops. Must not be used with bleach-based cleaners as chloride ions accelerate corrosion.</p>
<p>WorktopsBar topsSplashbacksBistro style</p>
